This Terraform configuration sets up a Route 53 latency-based routing policy that directs users to the nearest endpoint based on their geographic location. The configuration includes:

- A Route 53 hosted zone named "primary".
- Two Route 53 record sets for the zone, one for the US and one for the EU, each pointing to a different endpoint (e.g., an EC2 instance or a load balancer).
- A latency-based routing policy that routes traffic to the endpoint with the lowest latency based on the user's location.

The configuration assumes the use of AWS resources and includes a provider block with a default region.

---

**Terraform HCL Code:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "primary" {
  name = "primary.example.com."
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "us_endpoint" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.primary.id
  name    = "primary.example.com."
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = 300
  records = ["52.1.1.1"] # Replace with actual US endpoint IP
  set_identifier = "US_Endpoint"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "eu_endpoint" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.primary.id
  name    = "primary.example.com."
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = 300
  records = ["52.2.2.2"] # Replace with actual EU endpoint IP
  set_identifier = "EU_Endpoint"
